Hi all,

Sorry to trouble you but I wonder if anyone could provide some suggestions for solving my problem.
Following https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/heckman/stan-users/OfXuqdD2clA/mqiRD32YgP8J, I'm also working on a Heckman model.  But I got very small n_eff and 10000 divergent transitions after warmup. Rhat is around 1.0 but sd is very wide.
I have added control list to the code like this, fit = stan("heckman_hete.stan",data=data_list,iter=5000,chains=4,control=list(adapt_delta=0.99,stepsize=0.05, max_treedepth = 15)), but I still have a lot of divergent transitions.
Any suggestions will be much appreciated!

Best wishes!
Weijia

I'm using the same stan code as Mr/Mrs Jia provides in his/her discussion thread:
https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/heckman/stan-users/OfXuqdD2clA/mqiRD32YgP8J
data {
  int<lower=0> N;           // number of customers
  int<lower=0> M;           // total number of observations
  int<lower=0> nB1;         // number of beta in equation 1
  int<lower=0> nB2;          // number of beta in equation 2
  int<lower=0> nB;
  int<lower=0> nZ;           // number of individual demographic variables plus intercept
  int<lower=0> ID[M];        // index for the observations
  matrix[N,nZ] Z;
  matrix[M,nB1] X1;
  matrix[M,nB2] X2;
  vector[M] Y1;
  vector[M] Y2;
}
parameters {

  matrix[nB,nZ] mu;                           // prior matrix for beta
  matrix[nB,N] error_beta;                    // a trick on the variance of beta

  // cholesky_factor_corr[nB] L_Omega_beta;   // the correlation matrix of the two equations
  vector<lower=0>[nB] sigma_beta;             // the s.t.d of the second equation

  real<lower=-1,upper=1> rho_eq;              // the correlation matrix of the two equations
  real<lower=0> sigma_eq;                     // the s.t.d of the second equation

}
transformed parameters {

  matrix[N,nB] beta;
  // beta <- Z*mu' + (diag_pre_multiply(sigma_beta,L_Omega_beta)*error_beta)';
  beta <- Z*mu' + (diag_matrix(sigma_beta)*error_beta)';

}
model{

  matrix[N,nB1] beta1;
  matrix[N,nB2] beta2;

  vector[M] lp;
  vector[M] value;

//print("sigma_eq=",sigma_eq);
  sigma_eq ~ cauchy(0,2);                      // constrain it to be positive

//L_Omega_beta ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);       // correlation matrix of beta vector
//print("sigma_beta=",sigma_beta);
  sigma_beta ~ cauchy(0,2);                    // s.t.d. of the covariance matrix

  to_vector(mu) ~ normal(0,3);
  to_vector(error_beta) ~ normal(0,1);

  // transform beta for two equations for each customer
  beta1 <- block(beta,1,1,N,nB1);
  beta2 <- block(beta,1,nB1+1,N,nB2);

  // calculate the likelihood
  for( m in 1:M){
    if( Y1[m] == 0 ){
      lp[m] <- normal_cdf_log(-X1[m]*beta1[ID[m]]',0,1);
    }
    else{
      value[m] <- (X1[m]*beta1[ID[m]]'+rho_eq/sigma_eq*(Y2[m]-X2[m]*beta2[ID[m]]'))/(1-rho_eq^2)^0.5;
      lp[m] <- normal_cdf_log(value[m],0,1) - log(sigma_eq) - (Y2[m]-X2[m]*beta2[ID[m]]')^2/2/sigma_eq^2;
    }
  }

  increment_log_prob( log_sum_exp( lp ) );
}

Dear Bob,

Thank you very much for your reply.

With this model we aim to link the proportion of leaf area removed through invertebrate herbivory with some parameters like plant biomass. We have quite a lot of sample were we did not record any herbivory damage, therefore we have quite some 0s (8% of the samples). I would somehow feel uneasy if I had to drop that many observations ...

Concerning having alpha and beta (the two parameters for the beta distribution) vary per data point, I thought this was the standard approach in a beta regression (See here: https://github.com/mclark--/Miscellaneous-R-Code/blob/master/ModelFitting/Bayesian/rstanBetaRegression.R).

Finally for the model, I looked up for hurdle model in the reference manual, and would you agree that the following syntax would work:

(y[i] == 0) ~bernoulli(a);
if(y[i] > 0)
 increment_log_prob(log1m(a) + beta_log(alpha[i],beta[i]));

Yours,
Lionel

Hi all, I'm building a hierarchy model for revenue prediction  (model shown below). As you can see, model only consists of uninformative priors. The posterior for most of the beta's (beta_l2_1, beta_l2_2 etc.) all have some weird distributions while theoretically they should be gaussians. I was wondering whether that's because my posterior is mostly determined by the data as my prior is not very informative.

As a result, I want to start with a simple parameter scan to see whether the posterior distribution would change, such as restrict the uniform distribution of of my sigma's to have a narrow range (instead of   sigma_beta_l3_0 ~ uniform(0,100), try   sigma_beta_l3_0 ~ uniform(0,10),   sigma_beta_l3_0 ~ uniform(0,5) etc.) Is there anyway I could pass in the distribution parameter as a variable (such as, try 3 numbers of b, run the model 3 times with uniform(0, b) ) instead of writing 3 text files with different b's ?

Thank you in advance!
Maggie

data {
  int<lower=0> N;
  vector[N] y;
  vector[N] ant_spend;
  vector[N] unspent_ns;
  vector[N] cumul_revenue;
  int<lower=0> N_l1;
  int<lower=0> N_l2;
  int<lower=0> N_l3;
  int<lower=1> ff_l1_num[N];
  int<lower=1> ff_l2_num[N];
  int<lower=1> ff_l2_qtr_num[N];

  int<lower=0> N_new;
  vector[N_new] ant_spend_new;
  vector[N_new] unspent_ns_new;
  vector[N_new] cumul_revenue_new;
  int<lower=1> ff_l2_qtr_num_new[N_new];

  }
  parameters {
  real beta_0;
  real beta_1;
  real beta_2;
  real beta_3;
  real<lower=0> sigma_y;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l1_0;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l1_1;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l1_2;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l1_3;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l2_0;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l2_1;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l2_2;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l2_3;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l3_0;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l3_1;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l3_2;
  real<lower=0> sigma_beta_l3_3;
  vector[N_l1] beta_l1_0;
  vector[N_l1] beta_l1_1;
  vector[N_l1] beta_l1_2;
  vector[N_l1] beta_l1_3;
  vector[N_l2] beta_l2_0;
  vector[N_l2] beta_l2_1;
  vector[N_l2] beta_l2_2;
  vector[N_l2] beta_l2_3;
  vector[N_l3] beta_l3_0;
  vector[N_l3] beta_l3_1;
  vector[N_l3] beta_l3_2;
  vector[N_l3] beta_l3_3;

  }
  transformed parameters {
  vector[N] y_hat;
  for (i in 1:N)
  y_hat[i] <- beta_l3_0[ff_l2_qtr_num[i]] + beta_l3_1[ff_l2_qtr_num[i]] * ant_spend[i] + beta_l3_2[ff_l2_qtr_num[i]] * cumul_revenue[i] + beta_l3_3[ff_l2_qtr_num[i]] * unspent_ns[i];
  }

  model {
  sigma_y ~ uniform(0, 100);
  sigma_beta_l1_0 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l2_0 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l3_0 ~ uniform(0,100);
  beta_0 ~ normal(0,1);
  beta_l1_0 ~ normal(beta_0, sigma_beta_l1_0);
  for (n_l2 in 1:N_l2)
  beta_l2_0[n_l2] ~ normal(beta_l1_0[ff_l1_num[n_l2]], sigma_beta_l2_0);
  for (n_l3 in 1:N_l3)
  beta_l3_0[n_l3] ~ normal(beta_l2_0[ff_l2_num[n_l3]], sigma_beta_l3_0);

  sigma_beta_l1_1 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l2_1 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l3_1 ~ uniform(0,100);
  beta_1 ~ normal(0,1);
  beta_l1_1 ~ normal(beta_1, sigma_beta_l1_1);
  for (n_l2 in 1:N_l2)
  beta_l2_1[n_l2] ~ normal(beta_l1_1[ff_l1_num[n_l2]], sigma_beta_l2_1);
  for (n_l3 in 1:N_l3)
  beta_l3_1[n_l3] ~ normal(beta_l2_1[ff_l2_num[n_l3]], sigma_beta_l3_1);

  sigma_beta_l1_2 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l2_2 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l3_2 ~ uniform(0,100);
  beta_2 ~ normal(0,1);
  beta_l1_2 ~ normal(beta_2, sigma_beta_l1_2);
  for (n_l2 in 1:N_l2)
  beta_l2_2[n_l2] ~ normal(beta_l1_2[ff_l1_num[n_l2]], sigma_beta_l2_2);
  for (n_l3 in 1:N_l3)
  beta_l3_2[n_l3] ~ normal(beta_l2_2[ff_l2_num[n_l3]], sigma_beta_l3_2);

  sigma_beta_l1_3 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l2_3 ~ uniform(0,100);
  sigma_beta_l3_3 ~ uniform(0,100);
  beta_3 ~ normal(0,1);
